1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,060
Listen to a lecture in an Earth Science class.

2
00:00:03,060 --> 00:00:05,978
Today I'd like to explain the Mohs' scale,

3
00:00:05,978 --> 00:00:07,920
used in what is called the "scratch test

4
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:13,110
This scale is based on the simple fact that harder minerals scratch softer ones.

5
00:00:13,110 --> 00:00:17,580
For example, a diamond; a quartz crystal can scratch a feldspar crystal

6
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:19,170
but not the other around.

7
00:00:19,170 --> 00:00:25,100
The scale is named for Friedrich Mohs, the mineralogist who devised it in 1812.

8
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:28,840
His scale spans the range of minerals known at that time

9
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:31,000
from the softest to the hardest.

10
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,575
By performing a scratch test using known minerals and a few common tools,

11
00:00:35,575 --> 00:00:40,150
an unidentified mineral sample can be placed between two points on the scale.

12
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:44,120
By referring to the scale, the mineral can then be identified.

13
00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:48,574
I have here a collection of the minerals included on the Mohs' scale,

14
00:00:48,574 --> 00:00:52,000
as well as the tools necessary to complete this exercise.

15
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,079
I'd like you each to take a mineral sample from the basket at the front of the room

16
00:00:57,079 --> 00:01:00,020
and classify it according to its place on the Mohs' scale.

17
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:03,050
First, however, I should give you a little warning.

18
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:08,531
The hardness of any mineral depends on the strength of the bonds between ions or between atoms -

19
00:01:08,531 --> 00:01:11,110
the stronger the bond, the harder the mineral.

20
00:01:11,110 --> 00:01:14,867
Because bond strength may differ in various angles of a crystal,

21
00:01:14,867 --> 00:01:18,300
the hardness may vary slightly depending on the direction in

22
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:20,333
which the mineral sample is scratched,

23
00:01:20,333 --> 00:01:24,090
so be sure to scratch each sample in several different directions.
